 ## Silence guard
 
-A synthesis can collapse: the model stops speaking and generates silence until its end of speech or the frame limit. In the default voice that silence is near-digital: one measured reply did this for 64 s; another stayed silent for 37 s until the frame limit. It can also be a steady hum: one reply in a model-invented voice spoke for 1.5 s and hummed at about -58 dBFS until it ended at 45.2 s. The model can also stall and then recover: one reply was silent for 7.4 s and then spoke the rest of its text correctly.
+A synthesis can collapse: the model stops speaking and generates silence until its end of speech or the frame limit. In the default voice that silence is near-digital: one measured reply did this for 64 s; another stayed silent for 37 s until the frame limit. It can also be a steady hum: one reply in a model-invented voice spoke for 1.5 s and hummed at about -58 dBFS until it ended at 45.2 s, and the built-in voice, asked to speak `[quietly, almost whispering]`, said one word and hummed for 97 s. The model can also stall and then recover: one reply was silent for 7.4 s and then spoke the rest of its text correctly.
 
 So the engine decodes audio while it generates, every 4 frames for a stream and every 16 frames (0.74 s) otherwise. It ends a synthesis only once 10 s of frames in a row are below -40 dBFS (an RMS of 0.01), so a stall that has not recovered by then is treated as a collapse. Shorter stalls are kept as they are: the server does not trim them.
 
